Z||000000||Terrybogin||08-21-2000||11:27 PM||||I have a large collection of MP3's that I'd like to convert to an audio CD. Is that possible? What program would I use and where would I find it?<BR>Thanks-<BR>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000001||Blade||08-22-2000||01:01 PM||||cd creater 4...go to create audio cd then drag the mp3s to the other window. it will change them to the correct format.<P>Blade||||reg||1
Z||000002||87ECO5||08-22-2000||02:26 PM||||I think MusicMatch Jukebox 5.0 will do that too.  Actually, the cool thing is that some stereo manufacturers are now making their CD players read MP3 files.  So, you could just dump your MP3 files to a CD (without converting them) and get several hours of music on a single CD.<P>Ahhh, technology!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||olympic||08-22-2000||03:46 PM||||  You'll  also need lots of free hard drive space.  When you convert mp3's to .wav (the format readable by your CD player) they expand by like 10X.  A full CD worth of .wav's can take up nearly 1 GB.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||08-22-2000||04:36 PM||||But if you convert them to .wav files then burn them on a CD, couldn't you just delete the .wav files??  You'd have to copy them 1st before you converted them so you could save the original MP3s, but then you'd only take up the space for a short while.  What am I missing?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Blade||08-22-2000||07:47 PM||||If you use the cd creater 4 it converts for you from mp3. You dont need to convert to wav <BR>they expand onto the cd.<P>Blade||||reg||1
